{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's reliance on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to justify preference fails because Popper's own formal attempt to define verisimilitude was proven logically impossible by Tichý and Miller in 1974. They demonstrated that under Popper's definitions, a false theory with more content cannot be compared as 'closer to the truth' than another false theory without invoking inductive assumptions about which features are essential. Since the target claims the preference is defensible *on Popper's own terms* via verisimilitude, it rests on a concept Popper's framework could not formally sustain, thereby smuggling in the very inductive judgments (about relevant similarities) it seeks to avoid.","premise":"Tichý and Miller proved that Popper's definition of verisimilitude yields contradictory results when comparing two false theories, making non-inductive comparison of 'truth-likeness' impossible within his system.","premises_essential":["330d5bc4591d8cdcf71690c55147054e893fed61a10313eab1118c1b42329eec"],"successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot serve as the non-inductive basis for preference, does Popper have any remaining resource to explain why we should act on better-corroborated theories without implicitly assuming the future will resemble the past?","target":"330d5bc4591d8cdcf71690c55147054e893fed61a10313eab1118c1b42329eec"}]}